## Proof

For each element $i \in \{1,2,\dots,10\}$, define its **membership pattern** as the subset of $\{A_1, A_2, A_3\}$ to which $i$ belongs. There are $2^3 = 8$ possible membership patterns in total.

We translate the two conditions into constraints on membership patterns:

**(i) $A_1 \cup A_2 \cup A_3 = \{1,\dots,10\}$:** Every element must belong to at least one of the three sets. This forbids exactly one pattern — the one where the element belongs to **none** of $A_1, A_2, A_3$. This eliminates $1$ pattern, leaving $7$.

**(ii) $A_1 \cap A_2 \cap A_3 = \emptyset$:** No element may belong to all three sets simultaneously. This forbids exactly one pattern — the one where the element belongs to **all** of $A_1, A_2, A_3$. This eliminates $1$ pattern, leaving $6$.

The two forbidden patterns (belonging to none, belonging to all three) are distinct, so the number of admissible membership patterns per element is

$$8 - 1 - 1 = 6.$$

Crucially, the choices for different elements are **independent**: the membership pattern of element $i$ places no constraint on the membership pattern of element $j \neq i$, and any assignment of admissible patterns to all 10 elements produces a unique ordered triple $(A_1, A_2, A_3)$ satisfying both (i) and (ii). Conversely, every valid ordered triple arises from exactly one such assignment.

Therefore the total number of ordered triples is

$$6^{10} = (2 \cdot 3)^{10} = 2^{10} \cdot 3^{10}.$$

**Verification of the prime factorization form.** We have $6 = 2 \cdot 3$, so $6^{10} = 2^{10} \cdot 3^{10}$, with $a = 10$, $b = 10$, $c = 0$, $d = 0$. Numerically, $2^{10} \cdot 3^{10} = 1024 \cdot 59049 = 60{,}466{,}176$.

The answer, in the required form $2^a\, 3^b\, 5^c\, 7^d$, is

$$\boxed{2^{10}\, 3^{10}\, 5^{0}\, 7^{0}}.$$

